<Base title="Using a diagnostic code reference" description="A practical workflow for recording a scanner result, locating its reference and checking vehicle-specific documentation." breadcrumbs={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Guides'}]}><article class="container prose-page"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">FROM DISPLAY TO CONTEXT</p><h1>Read the code in context.</h1><p class="lede">A good lookup starts with the full scan report and ends with instructions that match the vehicle.</p></header><div class="prose"><section><h2>1. Keep the original result</h2><p>Save the complete display, module name, status and available freeze-frame data. Record the vehicle year, model, powertrain and the circumstances in which the warning appeared.</p></section><section><h2>2. Find the reference</h2><p>Use the exact code in the <a href="/search/">code finder</a>. Manufacturer-labelled definitions and generic references are separate. A similar-looking code can point to a different entry.</p></section><section><h2>3. Check the evidence</h2><p>Read the source label and the scope. A community code table is a useful locator; it does not establish an exact vehicle diagnostic procedure.</p></section><section><h2>4. Decide the next safe step</h2><p>Follow the vehicle’s warning messages and manufacturer documentation. Preserve evidence before clearing codes, and use qualified help where a procedure or safety boundary is unclear.</p></section><p><a href="/tools/">Scan-report checklist</a> · <a href="/safety/">Safety boundaries</a> · <a href="/models/">Documented vehicle references</a></p></div></article></Base>
